Terraform Advanced Interview Questions ⚡

Terraform is one of the most powerful Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tools. Once you’re comfortable with the basics, these advanced concepts and scenarios will help you stand out in interviews and real-world DevOps projects.

✨ Here are some of the most commonly asked advanced Terraform interview questions for candidates with 3–6 years of experience.

How does Terraform handle dependency resolution internally? Explain the difference between graph-based execution and linear execution.

Can you explain the difference between depends_on and implicit dependencies? When would you explicitly use depends_on?

How does Terraform manage resources when multiple providers are used in the same configuration?

Explain the difference between terraform.workspace and a directory-based environment structure. Which one would you prefer for multi-env setups and why?

What is the difference between for_each and count?

How does Terraform handle immutable and mutable infrastructure?

What are the limitations of Terraform state files in large projects? How do you mitigate state bloat and performance issues?

Explain how state locking works with backends like S3 + DynamoDB. What happens if a lock is not released?

When would you use terraform taint and terraform untaint? Provide real-world use cases.

How do you handle breaking changes in Terraform provider upgrades across multiple environments?

What are dynamic blocks in Terraform? Provide an example of when they are useful.

Explain the difference between locals, variables, and outputs. When should you use each?

What is a resource targeting in Terraform apply? Why is it considered risky?

How do you test Terraform code in CI/CD? Mention tools like Terratest, Checkov, or InSpec.

How do you implement policy-as-code with Terraform? Explain Sentinel or Open Policy Agent (OPA) integration.

What is drift detection in Terraform Cloud/Enterprise? How is it different from running terraform plan locally?

Explain the purpose of the lifecycle meta-arguments (create_before_destroy, prevent_destroy, ignore_changes).

How do you design reusable Terraform modules for multi-cloud infrastructure?

How does Terraform handle rollbacks if an apply partially fails? What strategies can you use to ensure safe deployments?

What are some best practices for structuring Terraform code in a monorepo and multi-repo setup?

